\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 ## Nonces and User Permissions Needed for Security
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Please add a nonce check to your input calls ($_POST, $_GET, $REQUEST) to prevent unauthorized access.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 If you use\'a0wp_ajax_\'a0to trigger submission checks, remember they also need a nonce check.\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \'a0Checking permissions: Keep in mind, a nonce check alone is not bulletproof security. Do not rely on nonces for authorization purposes. When needed, use it together with\'a0current_user_can()\'a0in order to prevent users without the right permissions from accessing things they shouldn't.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Also make sure that the\'a0
\f0\b nonce logic is correct by making sure it cannot be bypassed
\f1\b0 . Checking the nonce with\'a0current_user_can()\'a0is great, but mixing it with other checks can make the condition more complex and, without realising it, bypassable, remember that\'a0
\f0\b anything
\f1\b0 \'a0can be sent through an input,\'a0
\f0\b don't trust any input
\f1\b0 .\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Keep performance in mind. Don't check for post submission outside of functions. Doing so means that the check will run on every single load of the plugin, which means that every single person who views any page on a site using your plugin will be checking for a submission. This will make your code slow and unwieldy for users on any high traffic site, leading to instability and eventually crashes.\cb1 \
\
